\section{Derivations}
\label{app:deriv}

\paragraph{Leverage.}
Let $X$ be the $n\times k$ design with an intercept and $\varphi_i$ as rows and $\hat\tau=\Delta x^{\top}(X^{\top}X)^{-1}X^{\top}y=w^{\top}y$. Then $w=X(X^{\top}X)^{-1}\Delta x$ and $\|w\|^2=\Delta x^{\top}(X^{\top}X)^{-1}\Delta x$. Because $\Delta x$ has no intercept component, only the covariance $\hat\Sigma$ of the non-constant covariates enters, and $L=n\|w\|^2=\Delta x^{\top}\hat\Sigma^{-1}\Delta x$. For $\varphi=(z,f)$ with $z\perp f$, $\hat\Sigma\approx\mathrm{diag}(p(1-p),\mathrm{Var}f)$ with $\mathrm{Var}f=p(1-p)\,\overline{d_i^{-1}}\approx p(1-p)/d$, and $\Delta x=(1,1)$ gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:leverage}. With heterogeneous degrees, $d$ in \eqref{eq:leverage} is the harmonic mean of the degrees; in the experiments $L$ is computed from $w$ directly and the formula is only the reference.

\paragraph{Bias.}
For $Y_i=a_i+(1-s)z_i+s\,g(f_i)$ with $z\perp f$, the population OLS coefficients are $\beta_z=1-s$ and $\beta_f=s\,\mathrm{Cov}(g(f),f)/\mathrm{Var}(f)$, so $\beta_z+\beta_f-1$ is the first expression of Eq.~\eqref{eq:bias}. With $f=\mathrm{Bin}(d,p)/d$, a Taylor expansion of $g$ about $p$ gives $\mathrm{Cov}(g(f),f)/\mathrm{Var}(f)=g'(p)+\tfrac12 g''(p)(1-2p)/d+\dots$, because the third central moment of $f$ divided by its variance is $(1-2p)/d$. The same expansion of $g(1)-g(0)=1$ about $p$ gives $1=g'(p)+\tfrac12g''(p)(1-2p)+\dots$. Subtracting gives $\mathrm{bias}\approx-\tfrac s2g''(p)(1-2p)(1-1/d)$, which is the second expression of \eqref{eq:bias} for large $d$. These are local approximations in $d$; we do not claim them for thresholds, for which the exact first expression is used.
For mechanism (b), $g=c_i/\bar d$ with $c_i=d_if_i$. Under Bernoulli assignment the covariance of $(d_i/\bar d)f_i$ with $f_i$ is $p(1-p)/\bar d$ for each unit, and the population variance of $f$ is $p(1-p)\,\overline{d_i^{-1}}=p(1-p)/\mathrm{HM}(d_i)$, so $\beta_f=s\,\mathrm{HM}/\mathrm{AM}$ and the bias is $s[\mathrm{HM}/\mathrm{AM}-1]$.

