\paragraph{Confirmatory test of the noise-adjusted score.}
The noise-adjusted score $S_{\rm adj}$ with the corner-times-curvature term was tested once on a block of new cells, with its coefficients frozen on the 2142 original cells before the block ran: eight mechanisms (a)--(h), new seeds and graph draws, and two new graph families (Holme--Kim and log-normal degrees), 666 cells in 10 groups (the eight mechanisms and the two families). Its absolute out-of-sample $R^2$ for log$|{\rm bias}|$ is 0.115 against the mean of the block (0.157 against the frozen training mean), above zero. Its gain over the best of three degree benchmarks (chosen on the block, which favours the benchmark), $p(1-p)$ with $R^2$ 0.016, is 0.099 with a group-resampling interval of $[-0.059, 0.170]$; the rule needs a gain of at least 0.05 with the interval above zero, and positive gains in 7 of 10 groups, which hold in 5. The test is inconclusive, and $S_{\rm adj}$ is not cited as evidence. On the original cells the same score has held-out $R^2$ $-0.094$ leaving out mechanisms. The $\bar S$ specified before the runs gains 0.088 $[-0.077, 0.154]$ and the corner-times-curvature term alone 0.055 $[-0.144, 0.134]$ on the block. The block does not test the account that the bias is not identified from data inside the observed exposure range, and it does not change the verdict of Section~\ref{sec:diag}.

\paragraph{An interval widened by a bound on the bias.}
The interval is $\hat\tau\pm c_\alpha(\kappa B/\mathrm{SE})\,\mathrm{SE}_{\rm HC0}$ at the 90\% level, with $c_\alpha(b)$ the $1-\alpha$ quantile of $|N(b,1)|$, and $B$ one of two bounds on the extrapolation: $B_1$, the range of $\hat\tau$ over the five specifications of the ladder (regression, quadratic, spline, count and two-hop), or $B_2=\tfrac12\hat M[(1-p)^2+p^2]$, with $\hat M$ the larger, over the two own-treatment arms, of the largest second derivative of a cubic in $f$ fitted in the arm, over the observed range of $f$ of the draw (noise level 0.5). The multiplier $\kappa$ was frozen on the original cells before the block ran, as the smallest value of a grid from 0.25 to 64 at which the smooth mechanisms (a), (b), (c), (e), (f) are covered at least 90\% of the time: $\hat\kappa=0.25$ for $B_2$, the floor of the grid, and 1 for $B_1$. At the floor the grid cannot say how much smaller a $\kappa$ would still cover, so $B_2$ is a bound and not a calibration. On the 666 cells of the block (mechanisms (a)--(h); the 33 null-column cells are reported separately), $B_2$ covers 0.990 on average over cells (worst mechanism, (e): 0.935) at a mean width of 8.4, and $B_1$ 0.963 (worst 0.853) at 3.3, against 0.70 for HC0 at a width of 0.68, in units of $\gate$. The 1296 original cells, which were analysed after the results, give 0.981 at 11.0, 0.945 at 3.1 and 0.67. Where $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}>2$ (92 block cells) the coverage is 0.938 for $B_2$, 0.821 for $B_1$ and 0.153 for HC0. The width is smallest in that stratum (3.8 for $B_2$ against 11.6 at $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}$ 0.25--0.5), where the bias is largest (Figure~\ref{fig:p6}). The median $\kappa^\star$, the smallest multiplier that covers, over the smooth mechanism--degree cells is 0.25 for $B_2$, within the expected bound of 2, which a bound at the floor meets trivially. The expectation that on the threshold mechanism with the threshold outside the observed exposures (27 block cells) it would need a multiplier of at least 4 (eight times the smooth median) failed: $\kappa^\star$ is 0.25 for $B_2$, and coverage is 0.998; for $B_1$ it is 0.79 at $\kappa=0.25$ and 0.98 at $\kappa=1$. As a test of whether the width lands where the bias is, coverage minus that of HC0 inflated to the same mean width, averaged over 24 groups (mechanism by family) with a group-resampling interval, is $-0.010$ $[-0.020, -0.002]$ for $B_2$ against one global inflation and $-0.009$ $[-0.018, -0.002]$ against an inflation by family, degree and $p$; for $B_1$ against the latter it is $-0.007$ $[-0.018, 0.008]$. The rule (a gain of at least 0.03, interval above zero, in two thirds of the nine groups of the 585 cells without the threshold mechanism, two families and seven mechanisms; the worst group, (e), covers 0.949 for $B_2$ and 0.882 for $B_1$) fails for every bound and control, although the added width does track $|{\rm bias}|$ somewhat (within-mechanism rank correlation 0.31 for $B_2$ and 0.36 for $B_1$). On the original cells with a weak baseline (72) or edge noise (108) the coverage is 0.944 and 0.970 for $B_2$ and 0.828 and 0.887 for $B_1$. Only Bernoulli designs were run, because HC0 is a unit-level interval. The results concern these two bounds and the allocations of width tried; they do not say that no better interval exists, and a design that varies $p$ across units so that the corners enter the support is not tested. A diagnostic found after the failure of the expectation for the threshold mechanism shows why $B_2$ cannot tell a smooth response from a curved one: for the zero-curvature mechanisms (a) and (f) $\hat M$ is, on the original cells, about 0 without noise and 6.9, 26 and 112 at $d=5,20,80$ at the noise of the runs, an amplification of noise over the narrow range of $f$, so the bound is wide everywhere and grows with $d$. This is a property of the estimator and is consistent with the non-adaptivity of honest intervals \citep{armstrong2020simple}; we did not test that link. An exploratory arm run after these results replaces $\hat M$ in $B_2$ on the 585 block cells without the threshold mechanism (outside the verdict, $\hat\kappa$ not re-frozen; the infeasible arms use the truth, the treatment part alone of the same draw, or a noise-only column of it). The median $\hat M$ is 7, 25 and 109 at $d=5,20,80$, against 7.0, 25.5 and 110.5 for a column of the same draw that is noise alone, and 4.7 at most for the true supremum of $|g''|$ over $[0,1]$ in mechanisms (c), (g), (h); which is zero for (a) and (f) and also for (b) and (e), whose outcomes are linear in the assignment, so that no curvature bound in $f$ can cover their bias, and the threshold mechanism (d) has none. Pooled over the seven mechanisms other than (d), including (b) and (e) where the bound is empty, with the true $M$ the interval has a mean width 0.9 times that of the oracle ($\mathrm{HC0}+|\mathrm{bias}|$) at $\kappa=0.25$ and 1.7 times at $\kappa=1$, against 8.7 and 33 for the cubic, and covers 0.824 and 0.845; the same width of inflated HC0 covers 0.825 and 0.969. On the 423 block cells of mechanisms with a finite true $M$ it covers 0.913 at $\kappa=0.25$ at 1.04 times the oracle width (inflated HC0: 0.900), the smallest value of the grid that reaches 0.90, and 0.942 at $\kappa=1$ (2.2 times; inflated HC0: 0.999); on the 648 original cells of those mechanisms it covers 0.885 at 1.0 times and 0.895 at $\kappa=1$ (1.8 times), and does not reach 0.90 on the grid. It covers 0.591 of the 162 block cells of (b) and (e), where the bound is zero at every $\kappa$ (0.61 times the oracle width); no curvature bound reaches 0.90 there with the true $M$, and the cubic reaches it (0.966) only at 6.7 times the oracle width, through noise. A cubic with the noise removed (the same fit on the treatment part alone) gives 0.857 at $\kappa=0.25$ at 1.15 times the oracle width, and reaches 0.90 on the smooth block cells at $\kappa=0.5$ (1.2 times). A cubic less that of the noise-only column of the same draw, the one arm that approaches a feasible estimate, gives 0.794 at 0.93 times at $\kappa=0.25$ and does not reach 0.90 on the smooth block cells (0.877 at 31 times): subtracting the noise curvature removes signal with it. So most of the width of $B_2$ is the cost of estimating $M$ from noisy outcomes, and with the true $M$ the width needed on the smooth block cells is at most about the oracle's ($\kappa^\star$ is the grid floor), while on the original cells 0.90 is not reached; the cost of a feasible $M$ is not measured; the narrow versions do not cover where the bias comes from the wrong exposure variable and not from curvature in $f$; the arms that reach the oracle's width are not feasible, as they use the truth or the treatment part alone.

