\paragraph{Holme--Kim and log-normal graphs on the threshold mechanism.}
On mechanism (d) at $p=\tfrac12$ the Holme--Kim graph (heavy tail with clustering 0.12, one run) behaves like Barab\'asi--Albert: at $d=20$ and $s=0.5$ the regression's bias is 0.67 and 0.68 and its log RMSE ratio to DM 0.33 and 0.34, against 0.20--0.25 and $-0.89$ to $-0.62$ on the two real graphs. Log-normal degrees lie between them at $d=20$ (bias 0.33, log ratio $-0.35$, exposure SD 0.18) and not at $d=80$ (realised mean degree 73.6; bias 0.54, log ratio 0.18). The rank correlation of the exposure SD with the bias, over runs, is $-0.95$ on nine runs at $d=20$ (the stochastic block model counted twice, once from each source) and $+0.21$ on seven at $d=80$. Each family is one graph and one run, the correlations are exploratory, and the change of sign says that the spread of the exposures does not explain the bias in general; neither value bears for or against a mechanism. The tree-like form of the count-response bias (Section~\ref{sec:varbias}) predicts the bias on the 27 new Holme--Kim, log-normal and SBM runs of mechanism (b), unfitted, with correlation 0.999 and a largest gap of 0.009 (Holme--Kim $-0.141$, $-0.163$, $-0.129$ against $-0.147$, $-0.164$, $-0.127$ at $d=20,5,80$).

\paragraph{Graph neural network.}
A two-layer mean-aggregation network (hidden width 16) on the observed graph, with the node's own treatment, the mean neighbour treatment and the standardised log degree as inputs; weight decay and epochs were chosen by a graph-cluster hold-out with a corner loss, as for the other Tier A learners, and the estimate is the difference of its predictions at $(z,f)=(1,1)$ and $(0,0)$. It ran on Tier A cells with Bernoulli assignment, noise 0.5 and spillover share 0.5, mechanisms (a)--(f) and the null column, on the same assignments and graphs as the other learners but the first 10 of the 40 assignments of each of five graph draws (50 draws; on the real graphs the first 50 of 200 assignments), which every estimator in the comparison uses. The SBM cell with $d=80$ and $p=0.25$ did not finish (about four hours per cell) and is absent; 13 cells give 91 cell--mechanism rows. Median RMSE (normalised error) over the rows is 0.22 for the regression, 0.33 for the network, 0.40 for boosting, 0.40 for the adaptive H\'ajek, 0.42 for forests, 0.46 for lasso, 0.50 for DM and 0.62 for ridge (the adaptive H\'ajek is defined in 84 of the 91 rows, and its median and rank are over those); mean ranks (1 is best) are 1.8 for the regression, 3.4 for the network, then 4.4 to 6.1 for the others, and the regression is best in 59 rows, DM in 18 and the network in 2. The paired log RMSE ratio of the network to DM has an interval below zero in 57 rows and above in 27 (median $-0.25$), and to the regression below in 5 and above in 66 (median $+0.37$); the five rows below are cells of mechanism (d), where the mean log ratio to the regression is $-0.10$. On the null column the network is worse than DM (mean log ratio $+1.69$). These results concern one architecture without tuning of its width or depth; a larger or differently built network is not excluded, and it was not run on Tier G or on cluster designs.

