\paragraph{Acceptance rule.}
Table~\ref{tab:accept} gives the acceptance rule specified before the runs (Section~\ref{sec:diag}), criterion by criterion. The diagnostic is supported if (1) the pooled held-out gain of the combined score is at least 0.05 with the interval above zero and stays so after deleting any one group, (2) the gain is positive for at least 4 of 6 held-out mechanisms, (3) part (i) beats the benchmarks on the cells D$^*$ where $L$ and degree disagree and part (ii) beats them for the bias, and (4) within cells the rank correlation of $S$ with the absolute centred error exceeds that of a noise-only null and of the bootstrap SE. Part (i) selects D$^*$ on one half of the assignments of each graph draw and tests on the other. In part (ii), $\bar S$ and the curvature term use a random half of the draws of a cell and the coefficients of the score are fitted on the other half; taking the bias target from that half alone moves held-out $R^2$ by 0.02 at most. The absolute held-out $R^2$ of log RMSE on the 2178 cells is, leaving out mechanisms, 0.32 [$-0.13$, 0.73] for the combined score, 0.29 [0.14, 0.43] for the best degree benchmark, 0.25 [$-0.25$, 0.71] for leverage alone, 0.011 [$-0.06$, 0.08] for $\log n$ and $\log\sigma^2$ and $-0.049$ [$-0.069$, $-0.022$] for a constant; leaving out families, 0.34, 0.35, 0.25, 0.07 and $-0.03$. The combined score thus does not clearly improve on the degree benchmark for the variance either, and the benchmark itself explains part of the variation across cells.

