\section{Experimental grid and calibration}
\label{app:grid}

\paragraph{Grid.}
The frozen list covers: (R1) the core SBM, $n=3000$, $d\in\{5,20,80\}$, $p\in\{0.1,0.25,0.5,0.75,0.9\}$, all six mechanisms, $s\in\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75\}$ and noise multiples $\{0.25,0.5,1\}$; (R2) SBM with $n\in\{1000,10000\}$ at $p=\tfrac12$; (R3) ER, BA and WS graphs, $n=3000$, $p\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$; (R4) baselines correlated with degree or community; (R6) the two real graphs thinned to $d=5$, $20$ and $80$ (Stanford3, mean degree 98) or to $d=5$, $20$ and its full degree 44 (ego-Facebook, target 80), $p\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$; (R7) cluster designs on the SBM (planted partition and Louvain at $\gamma=0.3,1,3$), ER and BA at $d=20$; (R8) edge noise $q\in\{0.1,0.3\}$; (R9) a no-baseline, no-noise sanity run. The runs reported also include cells added after the freeze: a degree-corrected SBM, low-baseline runs, a fine grid in $s$, a fine grid in $p$ at $d=20$, the convex mirror (g), a weak-mixing SBM and cluster designs on the real graphs, and the noise multiples $\sigma=2,4$.

\paragraph{Cell populations.}
A cell is a run (graph family, size, degree, design, treated share, baseline) crossed with a mechanism, a spillover share $s$ and a noise level; the counts below are at $\sigma=0.5$ unless stated, and ``(a)--(f)'' are the six planned mechanisms, (g) the convex mirror. \emph{2193} (Table~\ref{tab:regime}, 126 runs): $s\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$, (a)--(f), Bernoulli and cluster designs, without the fine-$s$ and $p$-grid runs. \emph{1797} (its Bernoulli rows, 104 runs); the other 396 are cluster cells. \emph{2178} (Figure~\ref{fig:tiers}, 121 runs, and the absolute $R^2$ of log RMSE): the same cells without the 15 convex-mirror cells. \emph{1800} (Figure~\ref{fig:cov}, 94 runs): Bernoulli designs on the true feature graph, (a)--(f), $s>0$, with the fine-$s$ runs and without edge-noise, no-baseline sanity, convex-mirror and $p$-grid runs. \emph{1974} (oracle rule, 107 runs): every Bernoulli cell with $s>0$ and mechanisms (a)--(g), without the $p$-grid runs, so it includes the edge-noise, sanity and convex-mirror cells. \emph{1440} ($\hat s$ rule, 80 runs): the cells of the oracle rule that have an estimated share, 240 per mechanism (a)--(f); 36 are edge-noise or sanity cells, and without them the rule is right in 91.95\% of the remaining 1404 cells (91.8\% of 1440) and always using regression in 80.3\% (80.2\%). \emph{1224} (exact bias prediction, 68 runs): non-null cells (a)--(f) at $s\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}$ with an independent baseline, the true graph and a Bernoulli design (204 per mechanism); there is none for cluster, edge-noise, community-baseline or fine-$s$ cells. \emph{9795} (Figure~\ref{fig:var}, 107 runs): 1959 Bernoulli cells with mechanisms (a)--(f) at each of five noise multiples ($0.25$ to $4$), without the $p$-grid runs.

