\paragraph{Predictions specified before the runs.}
Fixed before the runs: (P1) the log-log slopes of $\mathrm{SD}(\hgate)$ on $n$, $1{+}d$ and $p(1{-}p)$ are $-\tfrac12$, $+\tfrac12$, $-\tfrac12$ where the specification is correct; (P2) the bias follows \eqref{eq:bias} and, for a smooth response in $f$, does not depend on $d$; (P3) variance is smallest at $p=\tfrac12$ and the bias minimiser moves for asymmetric responses; (P4) graph-cluster designs reduce bias and effective sample size; (P5) a diagnostic computed from the experiment predicts the variance (through $L$) and the bias (through disagreement among specifications and the corner distance) better than trivial predictors.
